Sporejack
Ingredients: ELECTRONIC BODY MUSIC (EBM) + PROGRESSIVE TRANCE + TRIPPY, SURREAL & PSYCHEDELIC + Harmonica
Creative Catalyst: Rogue botanists who wired bio-galvanic electrodes to tropical canopy flora to drive analog step-sequencers.

Sporejack fuses the jackhammer, mechanized muscle of classic Electronic Body Music with the euphoric, sprawling structural builds of late-1990s progressive trance, sliced open by an unexpected lead voice: a heavily overdriven diatonic harmonica pushed through analog tape saturation and psychedelic ring modulators. It sounds simultaneously industrial and organic—a relentless, sweat-soaked march through concrete corridors, suddenly bathed in a humid, hallucinatory canopy mist.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 128–134 BPM. Relentless, chugging sixteenth-note EBM basslines locked to punchy, acoustic-layered 4/4 kick drums, counterbalanced by progressive trance's rolling percussion shuffles and sweeping hi-hat patterns.
- Harmonic Palette: Phrygian and Dorian modes steeped in tension; dark minor drones that hold steady across 32-bar phrases before expanding into bittersweet, psychedelic suspended chords (sus2, sus4) and microtonal pitch bends.
- Production Signatures:
  1. The Overdriven Green-Bullet Chain: Diatonic harmonicas miked with high-impedance vintage blues-harp microphones, driven hard into transistor preamps, and diffused across synchronized stereophonic tape delays.
  2. Bio-Galvanic CV Modulation: Synthesizer filter cutoffs, wavetable indexes, and LFO speeds are subtly guided by fluctuating microvolt currents harvested from live plant leaves.
  3. Trance-Gated Jack-Pumping: Massive, detuned supersaw chord washes chopped cleanly into rigid sixteenth-note stabs via external sidechain noise gates keyed to the drum sequencers.
- Dynamics: Extended, hypnotic plateaus that construct tension over six- to ten-minute runtimes; tracks hold an austere, physical groove before collapsing into cavernous, dub-heavy psychedelic breakdowns that rebuild into surging, cathartic climaxes.

Scene & Origin Lore
Sporejack was born in the autumn of 1997 inside a disused rooftop solarium belonging to the University of Basel’s botanical department, where two laboratory technicians spliced electrode readouts from climbing Amazonian vines directly into the CV inputs of a Korg MS-20 and an Akai S1000 sampler. Frustrated by dry academic data, they dragged an amplifier and an overdriven Hohner Marine Band harmonica onto the gravel roof to jam along to the erratic bio-currents during thunderstorm nights. Word spread into the Swiss and South German squat scene, birthing an insular subculture nicknamed "Greenhouse Jackers." Dressed in rubberized laboratory aprons, steel-toed boots, and surplus ballistic goggles fitted with diffraction grating lenses, partygoers gathered in clandestine, ultra-humid industrial greenhouses. The central ritual involved "leaf-grounding": dancers rubbed sweat onto the sensory leads attached to hanging bromeliads, altering the synthesizer arpeggios and harmonica delay frequencies in real time through communal bodily conductivity.

Typical Instruments: Hohner Marine Band and Special 20 harmonicas (amped through Shure 520DX Green Bullet mics), Korg MS-20, Roland JP-8000, Roland TB-303, custom Plant-to-MIDI galvanic interface boxes, Roland RE-201 Space Echo, and Oberheim Matrix-1000.

For Fans Of: Front 242, Underworld, The Future Sound of London, Sasha & John Digweed (late-90s era).

